WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:11.280
Most people who use Claude or ChatGPT never trained it. You can use a model somebody else trained. This booklet lets you try that with our counting model.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:20.740
Here's the grid from The magpie. Fold it into a booklet, and every row becomes an entry. The empty boxes just fall away.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:38.827
Take the entry for "dog". On the grid you'd share the die's faces: nought to four for a full stop, five to nine for "runs". The booklet just writes where each band ends. Four. Nine.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:47.680
So roll, and take the first word your roll doesn't go past. Roll a three: that's not past four, so it's a full stop.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:01:07.740
The entry for "the" runs all the way to ninety-nine. That means two rolls, read as one number. Roll seven, then two. Seventy-two: past fifty-seven, not past seventy-eight. "Dog".

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:18.620
Now the booklet on your table. It came from a much bigger book. Start from "sleep": one die. Roll a seven: "again".

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:41.480
"Again" runs to ninety-nine, so two rolls. Eight, then nought: eighty. That's past seventy-six, not past eighty-four: "I'd". Roll a six: "float". And "float" has only one word after it, so no roll at all.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:54.240
Why a booklet, and not a grid? This book uses about four thousand different words. As a grid, that's sixteen million boxes. Only sixteen thousand of them have anything in them.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:02:06.360
As you go, listen to what comes out. There are more words to choose from, but each choice still uses only the last word. Try to guess the book before you check the cover.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:19.720
You can read every number in this model and run it at the kitchen table. An open-weights model makes its learned numbers available for people to download. With the software and hardware to run it, you can use your own copy.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:27.770
Grab your booklet and start rolling. And if you'd like to make your own from any text, the tool's at llmsunplugged.org/tools.
